1
00:01:51,083 --> 00:01:52,375
Morning, Your Grace.

2
00:01:54,750 --> 00:01:56,790
- Geoffrey.
- Enjoying the views?

3
00:01:56,791 --> 00:01:57,666
Mm.

4
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:01,165
Shouldn't you be headed back
to the house soon, sir?

5
00:02:01,166 --> 00:02:03,625
Your guests will be coming
down the stairs for breakfast.

6
00:02:04,125 --> 00:02:07,750
And I would like to start
the shoot team talk at 9:30.

7
00:02:09,208 --> 00:02:13,291
400 years ago, this estate spread out
as far as the eye could see.

8
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:16,333
<i>In every direction.
All the way down to the coast.</i>

9
00:02:17,708 --> 00:02:18,708
<i>It's been diminished.</i>

10
00:02:20,041 --> 00:02:24,124
<i>Year after year.
Generation after generation.</i>

11
00:02:24,125 --> 00:02:25,958
First came the apologies.

12
00:02:26,791 --> 00:02:28,291
Then came the shame.

13
00:02:30,333 --> 00:02:31,875
Then came the contraction.

14
00:02:33,791 --> 00:02:34,875
It's like a rot.

15
00:02:37,833 --> 00:02:39,166
I want it back, Geoff.

16
00:02:43,250 --> 00:02:44,583
I want it all back.

17
00:02:54,250 --> 00:02:56,374
So, we've bought this airfield?

18
00:02:56,375 --> 00:02:58,332
We haven't. His Lordship has.

19
00:02:58,333 --> 00:03:00,125
Through a shell company.

20
00:03:00,625 --> 00:03:01,707
100,000 square foot

21
00:03:01,708 --> 00:03:03,833
<i>of weed farm hidden under the hangar.</i>

22
00:03:04,333 --> 00:03:06,000
<i>Perfect cover for distribution.</i>

23
00:03:06,500 --> 00:03:08,832
<i>You grow it under the hangar.
Load in the hangar.</i>

24
00:03:08,833 --> 00:03:12,249
<i>And then you can distribute to anywhere
in the country by plane or road.</i>

25
00:03:12,250 --> 00:03:14,790
Then there's the air shows,
the restaurant, the gift shop.

26
00:03:14,791 --> 00:03:16,625
All good ways to clean money.

27
00:03:17,750 --> 00:03:19,457
Espresso martini, sir?

28
00:03:19,458 --> 00:03:21,540
Apparently, they start early
on a shoot day.

29
00:03:21,541 --> 00:03:23,416
In that case, when in Rome…

30
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:25,958
Madam?

31
00:03:28,750 --> 00:03:29,708
You know what?

32
00:03:30,458 --> 00:03:32,957
Dad might've been right
about the Duke after all.

33
00:03:32,958 --> 00:03:35,915
It was an inspired idea to mirror
what we're doing here in Italy.

34
00:03:35,916 --> 00:03:39,124
<i>Growing our product
underneath aristocratic palazzos.</i>

35
00:03:39,125 --> 00:03:40,415
<i>Speaking of which, here comes</i>

36
00:03:40,416 --> 00:03:43,499
our Italian connection,
who's our gateway into Europe.

37
00:03:43,500 --> 00:03:46,000
We've gone international,
and it's paying off.

38
00:03:46,500 --> 00:03:47,957
What's with the Spitfires?

39
00:03:47,958 --> 00:03:50,833
You never know when a war's
going to start, Jack.

40
00:04:09,916 --> 00:04:11,916
Good morning, Mr. Maldini.

41
00:04:12,500 --> 00:04:15,291
{\an8}<i>-Buongiorno.</i>
- I can take that for you.

42
00:04:16,750 --> 00:04:17,833
{\an8}Francesco.

43
00:04:18,458 --> 00:04:21,583
{\an8}- Good morning, Francesco.
- Uh, but y… you can call me Cico.

44
00:04:22,291 --> 00:04:23,125
{\an8}Cico.

45
00:04:25,666 --> 00:04:28,415
If you wanna follow me,
Blanket will take care of your bags.

46
00:04:28,416 --> 00:04:30,791
I'd follow you to the gates of St. Peter.

47
00:04:34,208 --> 00:04:35,375
Ciao, Susie!

48
00:04:36,875 --> 00:04:38,666
Susie, Susie, Susie.

49
00:04:39,333 --> 00:04:40,915
So good to see you.

50
00:04:40,916 --> 00:04:44,540
Look, there's something of a niggle
regarding our business dealings.

51
00:04:44,541 --> 00:04:47,124
It's something
that really needs to be discussed.

52
00:04:47,125 --> 00:04:49,625
We can talk about it later.
We're running a little bit late.

53
00:04:50,833 --> 00:04:54,082
- Contessa, you look divine.
- Thank you, Miss Glass.

54
00:04:54,083 --> 00:04:54,958
Shall we?

55
00:05:02,916 --> 00:05:05,332
Didn't fancy kedgeree, Lord Hawthorne?

56
00:05:05,333 --> 00:05:06,499
{\an8}Oh, no.

57
00:05:06,500 --> 00:05:09,207
{\an8}While I enjoy the flavor
of curry at breakfast,

58
00:05:09,208 --> 00:05:10,999
{\an8}it is impossible to trump the kipper.

59
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:12,749
Mm. I see you've met my nephew.

60
00:05:12,750 --> 00:05:15,291
I have indeed. Handsome little devil.

61
00:05:15,875 --> 00:05:17,041
Any word from the father?

62
00:05:17,708 --> 00:05:19,165
Hoping to join us for dinner.

63
00:05:19,166 --> 00:05:22,040
Well, it is wonderful
he works for a living.

64
00:05:22,041 --> 00:05:24,249
But he's just like Eddie.
He works too hard.

65
00:05:24,250 --> 00:05:26,500
Since when did a day of shooting
become work, mother?

66
00:05:28,375 --> 00:05:31,666
We're late.
But not too late for the breakfast.

67
00:05:34,125 --> 00:05:35,000
Eddie.

68
00:05:35,791 --> 00:05:36,915
Eduardo.

69
00:05:36,916 --> 00:05:38,249
Ed-weirdo.

70
00:05:38,250 --> 00:05:39,374
How are you, man?

71
00:05:39,375 --> 00:05:41,457
- Ciao.
- Good to see you. Come here.

72
00:05:41,458 --> 00:05:43,832
Oh.

73
00:05:43,833 --> 00:05:45,582
I'm so sorry,

74
00:05:45,583 --> 00:05:49,290
but my pilot decided
to fly into a fucking storm.

75
00:05:49,291 --> 00:05:50,875
Somehow we missed the eye.

76
00:05:52,791 --> 00:05:54,207
Well, you're here now.

77
00:05:54,208 --> 00:05:56,749
It wasn't a storm.
He was just being a baby.

78
00:05:56,750 --> 00:05:58,290
We haven't had the pleasure.

79
00:05:58,291 --> 00:06:00,374
So fucking formal.
I love this guy, I swear to God.

80
00:06:00,375 --> 00:06:03,207
This is my girlfriend,
Contessa Isabella Soranza de Parme.

81
00:06:03,208 --> 00:06:05,041
- Delighted to meet you.
- Your Grace.

82
00:06:06,583 --> 00:06:08,166
- Please, take a seat.
- Thank you.

83
00:06:10,708 --> 00:06:11,624
Hello.

84
00:06:11,625 --> 00:06:15,125
And how do you two
fit into this aristocratic jigsaw puzzle?

85
00:06:15,750 --> 00:06:18,832
Don't mind us. We're just here
to represent the proletariat.

86
00:06:18,833 --> 00:06:20,624
Oh, how very modern.

87
00:06:20,625 --> 00:06:23,707
Well, I must say,
you're two glorious-looking specimens.

88
00:06:23,708 --> 00:06:25,832
It's a brave new world, Your Lordship.

89
00:06:25,833 --> 00:06:28,707
Oh, what a wonderful accent.
Could you say that again?

90
00:06:28,708 --> 00:06:30,416
Come the revolution, brother.

91
00:06:31,041 --> 00:06:34,415
Guilty as charged. Spare me. Come, sit.

92
00:06:34,416 --> 00:06:36,208
Shall I have a word
about the language, sir?

93
00:06:36,708 --> 00:06:37,874
I think it would be prudent.

94
00:06:37,875 --> 00:06:39,832
Uh, I was thinking

95
00:06:39,833 --> 00:06:44,458
that maybe we could have
a little bit of chat with Susie, you know,

96
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:46,707
uh, before the bang-bang starts.

97
00:06:46,708 --> 00:06:49,208
Nothing major, you know?
Just a little bit of business.

98
00:06:51,833 --> 00:06:53,083
We'll deal with it later.

99
00:06:53,625 --> 00:06:55,707
Geoff would like us on the pegs by 9:30,

100
00:06:55,708 --> 00:06:58,541
and we wouldn't want
to disappoint him, now, would we?

101
00:07:00,250 --> 00:07:03,125
Don't worry, Cico. We've got all morning.

102
00:07:05,625 --> 00:07:06,625
Yeah.

103
00:07:08,041 --> 00:07:08,875
All right.

104
00:07:16,125 --> 00:07:18,457
<i>He's edgy, Susan. It's concerning me.</i>

105
00:07:18,458 --> 00:07:20,208
So, what is the issue with Cico?

106
00:07:20,708 --> 00:07:22,000
Yet to be determined.

107
00:07:22,583 --> 00:07:24,375
I'm sure it's nothing positive.

108
00:07:24,958 --> 00:07:26,790
I know you're not
his number one fan, Eddie,

109
00:07:26,791 --> 00:07:29,165
but he has helped us
get a foothold in Italy,

110
00:07:29,166 --> 00:07:32,165
a territory beset by monsters
around every corner.

111
00:07:32,166 --> 00:07:34,457
His efforts shouldn't be underestimated.

112
00:07:34,458 --> 00:07:37,207
Fixers are supposed to fix problems,
not create them.

113
00:07:37,208 --> 00:07:40,249
I say we give him the benefit of the doubt
for the time being.

114
00:07:40,250 --> 00:07:43,832
Meanwhile, you can focus your efforts
on snagging yourself a peerage,

115
00:07:43,833 --> 00:07:45,333
as per my father's request.

116
00:07:45,916 --> 00:07:47,625
You leave Lord Hawthorne to me.

117
00:07:54,750 --> 00:07:57,124
Hope we haven't got
too many plates spinning today.

118
00:07:57,125 --> 00:07:59,207
No. Shoots are never
about the killing, Susan.

119
00:07:59,208 --> 00:08:00,582
They're about the deal-making.

120
00:08:00,583 --> 00:08:03,624
It's an alibi to fuse
a collection of disparate rogues.

121
00:08:03,625 --> 00:08:06,333
Contrary to logic,
the more spinning plates, the better.

122
00:08:06,833 --> 00:08:10,040
People will fly 5,000 miles
for a shoot in the English countryside,

123
00:08:10,041 --> 00:08:11,707
and for some strange reason,

124
00:08:11,708 --> 00:08:14,207
are more open to business
when they've got a gun in their hand.

125
00:08:14,208 --> 00:08:15,207
Hm.

126
00:08:15,208 --> 00:08:18,124
- Tell me more about your friend.
- You'll like Aisha.

127
00:08:18,125 --> 00:08:20,165
Clever girl. Aspirational.

128
00:08:20,166 --> 00:08:22,999
Looks after herself
and everyone who comes within her orbit.

129
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:26,249
She moved to the Middle East
five years ago to work in hospitality.

130
00:08:26,250 --> 00:08:28,749
Now she's snagged herself
a seat at the royal table.

131
00:08:28,750 --> 00:08:30,541
The Middle East is the future, Susie.

132
00:08:31,375 --> 00:08:33,707
They build a new world out there
every two weeks.

133
00:08:33,708 --> 00:08:36,625
Yeah, I know why you want him here.
I like your ambition.

134
00:08:37,500 --> 00:08:39,916
Hello, honey. How are you doing?

135
00:08:40,625 --> 00:08:42,707
- This is the Duke.
- How do you do?

136
00:08:42,708 --> 00:08:45,083
I do very well. Thank you for hosting us.

137
00:08:45,666 --> 00:08:49,790
So this is His Excellency,
Prince Amir bin Abdullah al-Hadidi.

138
00:08:49,791 --> 00:08:50,625
Well.

139
00:08:51,291 --> 00:08:52,832
Good morning, Sheikh Amir.

140
00:08:52,833 --> 00:08:55,915
Your Grace. Thank you so much
for inviting us to your beautiful home.

141
00:08:55,916 --> 00:08:56,832
Of course.

142
00:08:56,833 --> 00:08:58,874
- I brought you a small gift.
- Ah.

143
00:08:58,875 --> 00:09:01,707
Double magnum of Petrus '09.
Very generous.

144
00:09:01,708 --> 00:09:04,540
Why do I get the feeling
you might have over-delivered?

145
00:09:04,541 --> 00:09:05,832
I mean, he's a sweetheart.

146
00:09:05,833 --> 00:09:08,832
Second in line to the throne.
Loves a good time.

147
00:09:08,833 --> 00:09:12,540
And he's got more money than…
Well, put it this way, he's fucking rich.

148
00:09:12,541 --> 00:09:14,207
Never mind me over-delivering.

149
00:09:14,208 --> 00:09:16,500
You never said about
your Duke looking like a movie star.

150
00:09:21,875 --> 00:09:25,125
- He speaks fucking Arabic?
- He gets better by the second.

151
00:09:35,083 --> 00:09:36,166
Your wife?

152
00:09:37,500 --> 00:09:38,625
Business wife.

153
00:09:39,125 --> 00:09:41,250
Amir, we must get you a gun.

154
00:09:44,875 --> 00:09:48,582
Why are there so many?
A… And why only two barrels?

155
00:09:48,583 --> 00:09:50,333
Give the birds a sporting chance, sir.

156
00:09:51,583 --> 00:09:54,791
- Which one is the best?
- The best? Or the most impressive?

157
00:09:55,625 --> 00:09:56,458
Both.

158
00:09:57,500 --> 00:10:01,082
You have your Germans,
your Brits, and your American guns.

159
00:10:01,083 --> 00:10:04,416
But if you're looking
for prestige and heritage,

160
00:10:04,916 --> 00:10:07,083
I'm feeling Italian Beretta for you, sir.

161
00:10:08,458 --> 00:10:10,208
They hunt in pairs.

162
00:10:11,125 --> 00:10:13,332
They've been at it for 500 years.

163
00:10:13,333 --> 00:10:15,500
Oldest gunmakers in the world.

164
00:10:16,000 --> 00:10:17,750
You see that engraving?

165
00:10:18,333 --> 00:10:22,250
That took a little Italian man two years
to etch that pretty picture.

166
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:26,000
It's magnificent.

167
00:10:26,500 --> 00:10:28,540
No good in a Chicago gunfight,

168
00:10:28,541 --> 00:10:31,875
but absolutely wonderful
in the green fields of England.

169
00:10:36,708 --> 00:10:40,208
- I feel an erection coming on.
- That's the appropriate response, sir.

170
00:10:41,791 --> 00:10:44,249
Look at that. Fits like a glove.

171
00:10:44,250 --> 00:10:46,332
James Bond's weapon of choice.

172
00:10:46,333 --> 00:10:48,582
I thought that was the Walter PPK, no?

173
00:10:48,583 --> 00:10:50,790
No, not in the original novel.

174
00:10:50,791 --> 00:10:53,041
I think you'll do very well
with those Berettas, Amir.

175
00:10:53,958 --> 00:10:54,875
Geoffrey.

176
00:10:57,083 --> 00:11:00,915
You'll remember to ensure Lord Hawthorne
has a suitably memorable experience?

177
00:11:00,916 --> 00:11:03,249
I'll make sure
his arse is drowning in the butter.

178
00:11:03,250 --> 00:11:05,665
- Good.
- Anything special for him, sir?

179
00:11:05,666 --> 00:11:06,958
Of course, Geoffrey.

180
00:11:08,125 --> 00:11:09,666
- Only the best.
- Oh!

181
00:11:10,791 --> 00:11:13,875
- Lord Monmouth's Holland & Holland, sir?
- Exactly.

182
00:11:14,875 --> 00:11:15,875
- Lucky man.
- Hm.

183
00:11:16,750 --> 00:11:18,791
That would be my weapon of choice, sir.

184
00:11:28,083 --> 00:11:31,457
Geoff, Geoff, Geoff. I'm sorry.
I don't want to be a pain in the ass,

185
00:11:31,458 --> 00:11:34,499
but, you know,
as much as I like my loader,

186
00:11:34,500 --> 00:11:38,750
I would like that angel from Essex
to load my gun for me, please.

187
00:11:39,250 --> 00:11:42,499
- I'll look into it.
- Yes! Okay, <i>grazie</i>. Fuck. Yes.

188
00:11:42,500 --> 00:11:44,125
You might wanna tone down the language.

189
00:11:45,833 --> 00:11:48,875
You're a guest of His Grace,
and there's ladies present.

190
00:11:49,666 --> 00:11:51,625
- Just a suggestion.
- All right.

191
00:11:52,666 --> 00:11:53,875
Okay, everyone.

192
00:11:54,416 --> 00:11:56,666
Your numbers will be
on the bottom of the glasses.

193
00:11:57,166 --> 00:11:59,000
That will be your peg number for the day.

194
00:11:59,583 --> 00:12:01,832
- Your Grace?
- Bottoms up.

195
00:12:01,833 --> 00:12:03,374
- Cheers.
- Chin-chin.

196
00:12:03,375 --> 00:12:04,416
Loaders?

197
00:12:18,583 --> 00:12:19,500
Good shot, sir.

198
00:12:41,833 --> 00:12:43,457
I don't understand how I'm missing.

199
00:12:43,458 --> 00:12:46,207
Don't worry, sir.
I'm sure you'll get them in the next one.

200
00:12:46,208 --> 00:12:48,832
I must say,
you're very good at this, Jack.

201
00:12:48,833 --> 00:12:52,125
Well, you can show me your appreciation
in your tip, sir.

202
00:12:53,625 --> 00:12:55,540
You've got to at least try
and hit one, mother.

203
00:12:55,541 --> 00:12:58,040
Darling, you know,
you've become very bloodthirsty

204
00:12:58,041 --> 00:12:59,958
since you left university.

205
00:13:01,291 --> 00:13:02,333
Forty.

206
00:13:02,833 --> 00:13:06,457
- Aisha! I want one of these.
- You want one of what?

207
00:13:06,458 --> 00:13:09,375
- One of these!
- Then you shall have one of these.

208
00:13:10,791 --> 00:13:12,000
Greedy cunt.

209
00:13:16,916 --> 00:13:17,833
Thank you.

210
00:13:22,625 --> 00:13:23,625
Thank you, sir.

211
00:13:25,333 --> 00:13:27,124
You shoot beautifully, Bella.

212
00:13:27,125 --> 00:13:29,291
Thank you. You shoot like a god.

213
00:13:33,458 --> 00:13:36,499
Jack, I must say,
you were absolutely wonderful out there.

214
00:13:36,500 --> 00:13:38,000
You put me right at ease.

215
00:13:38,583 --> 00:13:41,875
I tell you what, shall we be
very naughty boys and have one more?

216
00:13:42,458 --> 00:13:46,540
- You've read my mind, Your Lordship.
- Thank you, Jack.

217
00:13:46,541 --> 00:13:47,624
Eddie! Eddie.

218
00:13:47,625 --> 00:13:50,457
<i>How do I love thee?
Let me count the ways</i>

219
00:13:50,458 --> 00:13:52,332
What a wonderful drive.

220
00:13:52,333 --> 00:13:55,207
The birds just kept coming at me,
wave after wave of them.

221
00:13:55,208 --> 00:13:59,165
You did yourself proud, Lord Hawthorne.
Nothing much got past you.

222
00:13:59,166 --> 00:14:01,999
I did shoot rather well,
if I say so myself.

223
00:14:02,000 --> 00:14:03,457
You did indeed.

224
00:14:03,458 --> 00:14:05,790
Will you be heading
to Scotland this summer?

225
00:14:05,791 --> 00:14:07,374
Chance'd be a fine thing.

226
00:14:07,375 --> 00:14:09,332
Too much going on
in the corridors of power.

227
00:14:09,333 --> 00:14:12,582
Mm, yes. I heard about
Lord Bramlingham's death.

228
00:14:12,583 --> 00:14:14,750
- Wasn't it shocking?
- Shocking indeed.

229
00:14:15,250 --> 00:14:17,624
I suppose you'll be looking
to fill the position?

230
00:14:17,625 --> 00:14:21,166
It's not often that a spot
for a hereditary peer comes up.

231
00:14:21,958 --> 00:14:23,166
Any idea who might get it?

232
00:14:26,833 --> 00:14:29,666
We're drawing up a short list
this weekend.

233
00:14:30,833 --> 00:14:35,040
You know, you and I,
we should have a drink before dinner.

234
00:14:35,041 --> 00:14:37,916
I've always been fascinated
by the inner workings of Parliament.

235
00:14:39,625 --> 00:14:40,708
Have you, indeed?

236
00:14:41,208 --> 00:14:42,333
Mm.

237
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:47,166
Right. These birds
aren't gonna kill themselves.

238
00:14:59,500 --> 00:15:03,458
You might want to miss one or
two. You're making the Italian look bad.

239
00:15:04,250 --> 00:15:07,374
- Do we have a problem with that, Geoffrey?
- I don't, but I think his ego does.

240
00:15:07,375 --> 00:15:11,083
Oh. Well, we might have to lie
on the count, Geoff.

241
00:15:12,458 --> 00:15:14,416
Things have been good between us, yeah?

242
00:15:14,958 --> 00:15:16,874
This time last year, we had nothing.

243
00:15:16,875 --> 00:15:20,165
But since we started that little farm

244
00:15:20,166 --> 00:15:22,541
under the Contessa's palazzo estate,
my God!

245
00:15:23,291 --> 00:15:24,833
You guys nailed it.

246
00:15:25,750 --> 00:15:27,790
Six more farms in less than a year.

247
00:15:27,791 --> 00:15:30,165
You said there was a problem.
Why don't you enlighten us?

248
00:15:30,166 --> 00:15:34,583
Yeah. There is a real problem.
The problem is, uh, Marco Moretti.

249
00:15:35,250 --> 00:15:36,500
Sorry, what?

250
00:15:38,291 --> 00:15:39,166
Marco Moretti?

251
00:15:40,833 --> 00:15:42,875
What the fuck is Moretti doing
in this conversation?

252
00:15:43,541 --> 00:15:45,165
We need to talk to him.

253
00:15:45,166 --> 00:15:48,458
The reason we talk to you is so we don't
have to talk to El Capo Dei Capi.

254
00:15:50,041 --> 00:15:52,499
I know, but then
he started sniffing around.

255
00:15:52,500 --> 00:15:55,915
He found out we are growing marijuana
under the Contessa's palazzo.

256
00:15:55,916 --> 00:15:59,457
Marco is her godfather.
He was always going to find out.

257
00:15:59,458 --> 00:16:03,541
And now he wants a piece of it. Otherwise,
he's gonna rip it out of the ground.

258
00:16:06,500 --> 00:16:08,624
{\an8}Hold on. Hold on.
We're spiraling here, Cico.

259
00:16:08,625 --> 00:16:10,833
You pushed us out of an aeroplane
without a parachute.

260
00:16:11,416 --> 00:16:13,624
<i>You told us
you could keep Marco out of this.</i>

261
00:16:13,625 --> 00:16:16,249
Where's our Mr. Consistent?
Where's our Mr. Reliable?

262
00:16:16,250 --> 00:16:19,665
It's Italy.
Everything is a surprise, all right?

263
00:16:19,666 --> 00:16:22,624
This is why you have me. Okay?
So I can stop the crisis.

264
00:16:22,625 --> 00:16:25,082
How much to stop the crisis?

265
00:16:25,083 --> 00:16:25,916
Like,

266
00:16:27,250 --> 00:16:28,332
forty a year?

267
00:16:28,333 --> 00:16:29,166
Forty what?

268
00:16:29,666 --> 00:16:33,624
Forty lira? Forty winks? Forty lashes?
Forty nights in the desert?

269
00:16:33,625 --> 00:16:35,207
Forty fucking what, Cico?

270
00:16:35,208 --> 00:16:37,707
Forty million euros, obviously.

271
00:16:37,708 --> 00:16:40,290
- Okay, that's it. We're off.
- Okay, wait, wait, wait, wait.

272
00:16:40,291 --> 00:16:44,582
Okay. Fine. Obviously,
you know, 40 is unacceptable.

273
00:16:44,583 --> 00:16:46,707
- Mm.
- But this is why you pay me.

274
00:16:46,708 --> 00:16:48,957
I have a… a solution.

275
00:16:48,958 --> 00:16:51,332
Well, it's a pretty big
fucking problem, Francesco,

276
00:16:51,333 --> 00:16:54,082
so it better be a big fucking solution.

277
00:16:54,083 --> 00:16:59,000
Well, um, how familiar are you
with the works of Botticelli?

278
00:17:00,583 --> 00:17:02,624
<i>A small picture of his
is coming up for sale</i>

279
00:17:02,625 --> 00:17:04,500
<i>at an auction house in London.</i>

280
00:17:05,250 --> 00:17:08,707
<i>- Marco is obsessed with his work.
- It's a punchy ask.</i>

281
00:17:08,708 --> 00:17:11,791
Six million's not much
if it has the desired effect.

282
00:17:12,291 --> 00:17:14,957
Marco Moretti runs a serious organization.

283
00:17:14,958 --> 00:17:17,957
Extortion, kidnap, torture,
they're into all of it.

284
00:17:17,958 --> 00:17:20,665
And they've got heavyweight backing
from church and state.

285
00:17:20,666 --> 00:17:22,791
Look, I'm not questioning
the power of Marco Moretti.

286
00:17:24,041 --> 00:17:26,249
I understand the nuance and sophistication

287
00:17:26,250 --> 00:17:28,832
of buying a painting
by his favorite Renaissance artist.

288
00:17:28,833 --> 00:17:34,915
My question is whether or not Cico is
the most effective method to access him.

289
00:17:34,916 --> 00:17:38,000
I know you don't like him.
I like him even less than you do.

290
00:17:38,500 --> 00:17:41,416
But we've got to keep him on side.
At least in the short term.

291
00:17:41,916 --> 00:17:44,500
Anyway, you don't have to like
everyone you work with.

292
00:17:47,791 --> 00:17:49,083
I know who you do like.

293
00:17:50,708 --> 00:17:54,415
- What does that mean?
- It can mean whatever you want it to mean.

294
00:17:54,416 --> 00:17:56,791
Ten-minute warning,
everybody. Ten minutes.

295
00:18:12,041 --> 00:18:13,208
Thank you.

296
00:18:16,958 --> 00:18:18,041
Oh!

297
00:18:36,458 --> 00:18:37,541
Cico!

298
00:18:38,750 --> 00:18:39,665
Cico!

299
00:18:39,666 --> 00:18:42,583
Hold the line!

300
00:18:44,375 --> 00:18:46,874
Cico. Cico?

301
00:18:46,875 --> 00:18:48,290
Is he dead?

302
00:18:48,291 --> 00:18:50,499
Hm. He'll be all right.

303
00:18:50,500 --> 00:18:53,457
A partridge hit him on the head.
Doesn't usually kill 'em.

304
00:18:53,458 --> 00:18:54,416
"Usually"?

305
00:18:57,208 --> 00:18:59,916
Cico? Cico?

306
00:19:02,291 --> 00:19:04,874
The man's a buffoon.
We shouldn't be buying the art.

307
00:19:04,875 --> 00:19:07,499
If you're lucky,
he won't recover from the aerial assault.

308
00:19:07,500 --> 00:19:10,332
- Chicken tried to murder me.
- Focus on Lord Hawthorne.

309
00:19:10,333 --> 00:19:12,957
He's a cash-strapped lord
with a crumbling old pile.

310
00:19:12,958 --> 00:19:14,083
Shouldn't be too hard.

311
00:19:19,041 --> 00:19:19,916
Mm.

312
00:19:22,458 --> 00:19:27,833
I know I look like a useless old fruit,
but I do know when I'm being worked.

313
00:19:28,708 --> 00:19:32,125
The birds, the food, the Petrus.

314
00:19:32,625 --> 00:19:33,624
I get it.

315
00:19:33,625 --> 00:19:36,665
And I'm… …very grateful, Eddie.
Really, I am.

316
00:19:36,666 --> 00:19:38,750
But I… I know what you're up to.

317
00:19:39,375 --> 00:19:40,791
I'm being greased.

318
00:19:41,625 --> 00:19:44,083
I'm being lubed.

319
00:19:44,583 --> 00:19:48,416
In the hope that I might support
your efforts to become a hereditary peer.

320
00:19:49,541 --> 00:19:51,666
Well, you're an important man,
Lord Hawthorne.

321
00:19:52,500 --> 00:19:57,041
It's only fitting that you should receive
the appropriate embellishments.

322
00:19:57,916 --> 00:20:00,708
And I suppose the reason
you want this position

323
00:20:01,333 --> 00:20:04,624
is so that you can influence
a key piece of legislation

324
00:20:04,625 --> 00:20:07,958
proposing the decriminalization
of marijuana.

325
00:20:09,625 --> 00:20:11,041
Why would I care about that?

326
00:20:11,541 --> 00:20:13,249
I've no idea, Eddie.

327
00:20:13,250 --> 00:20:16,040
Whatever your reasons,
they are no concern of mine.

328
00:20:16,041 --> 00:20:19,165
No, what interests me is the number
you think I can be bought for,

329
00:20:19,166 --> 00:20:20,833
so let's not waste any more time.

330
00:20:23,541 --> 00:20:25,124
What number did you have in mind?

331
00:20:25,125 --> 00:20:28,374
He's a cash-strapped lord with a crumbling
old pile. Shouldn't be too hard.

332
00:20:28,375 --> 00:20:30,374
What are you thinking
of opening with? Two mil?

333
00:20:30,375 --> 00:20:32,332
Oh, God, no. Something like £100,000.

334
00:20:32,333 --> 00:20:33,666
Don't be ridiculous.

335
00:20:35,208 --> 00:20:37,333
£50,000?

336
00:20:38,750 --> 00:20:41,749
You'd be surprised what
political bureaucrats will do for cash.

337
00:20:41,750 --> 00:20:44,375
If you can pull that off,
next meal deal's on me.

338
00:20:46,083 --> 00:20:49,624
I am afraid that is
a little out of my league, Lord Hawthorne.

339
00:20:49,625 --> 00:20:52,624
Feel free to enjoy the rest of the Petrus.

340
00:20:52,625 --> 00:20:53,458
Thirty-five.

341
00:20:56,583 --> 00:20:57,540
Twenty-five.

342
00:20:57,541 --> 00:20:58,625
Thirty.

343
00:21:03,875 --> 00:21:07,374
{\an8}For that, I need your endorsement
of my nomination as a hereditary peer,

344
00:21:07,375 --> 00:21:09,540
along with the support
of the crossbenchers,

345
00:21:09,541 --> 00:21:13,082
who will happily go along with you
if you ask them nicely,

346
00:21:13,083 --> 00:21:17,207
thus guaranteeing me a seat
in the House of Lords.

347
00:21:17,208 --> 00:21:19,040
Any further assistance I may need

348
00:21:19,041 --> 00:21:21,582
in terms of influencing
specific legislation,

349
00:21:21,583 --> 00:21:25,208
well, that will be negotiated
on a case-by-case basis.

350
00:21:28,541 --> 00:21:30,583
Well, that sounds reasonable.

351
00:21:32,333 --> 00:21:37,500
Oh, there is one other provision
that I'm afraid I must insist on.

352
00:21:38,083 --> 00:21:39,416
And what's that?

353
00:21:40,000 --> 00:21:40,833
Jack.

354
00:21:44,125 --> 00:21:45,624
Uh, Jack?

355
00:21:45,625 --> 00:21:47,000
Jack the loader.

356
00:21:47,500 --> 00:21:48,999
I'm interested in him.

357
00:21:49,000 --> 00:21:51,958
Right. Interested in him in what way?

358
00:21:52,666 --> 00:21:54,708
Anthropologically.

359
00:21:55,291 --> 00:21:57,333
He's a rare creature, Eddie,

360
00:21:57,958 --> 00:21:59,249
who seems to possess

361
00:21:59,250 --> 00:22:05,000
<i>the wild vitality
of some long-forgotten primal man.</i>

362
00:22:09,375 --> 00:22:11,333
Well, I'm sure something can be arranged.

363
00:22:13,583 --> 00:22:14,500
Hm.

364
00:22:16,708 --> 00:22:17,915
Good evening.

365
00:22:17,916 --> 00:22:21,082
I hope you've enjoyed your day's sport
at Halstead Manor.

366
00:22:21,083 --> 00:22:22,958
Today's bag, sir.

367
00:22:23,541 --> 00:22:27,000
120 partridge, two wood pigeon,

368
00:22:27,625 --> 00:22:29,375
and one Italian businessman.

369
00:22:29,875 --> 00:22:31,290
The bad news is,

370
00:22:31,291 --> 00:22:35,666
Your Lordship gets the wooden spoon
for 94 shots and not a lot of feather.

371
00:22:36,250 --> 00:22:38,457
I blame my instructor, Geoffrey.

372
00:22:38,458 --> 00:22:40,749
I will have a word with him myself, sir.

373
00:22:40,750 --> 00:22:41,874
The good news is,

374
00:22:41,875 --> 00:22:44,707
Her Ladyship has redeemed the family name.

375
00:22:44,708 --> 00:22:48,499
She shot ten birds on the bounce,
and best dressed in the field.

376
00:22:48,500 --> 00:22:49,875
- Hear, hear.
- Thank you.

377
00:22:51,541 --> 00:22:54,666
Your Grace, Your Ladyship,
ladies and gentlemen.

378
00:22:55,166 --> 00:22:56,833
- Thank you.
- Thank you.

379
00:23:01,083 --> 00:23:04,958
I wanted to propose, um, a toast to

380
00:23:06,083 --> 00:23:08,707
Eduardo, the most generous host.

381
00:23:08,708 --> 00:23:12,290
It's so incredible
to be invited over to England

382
00:23:12,291 --> 00:23:19,000
to share in your ancient tradition
and your inspirational visions

383
00:23:20,000 --> 00:23:21,250
for a brighter future.

384
00:23:23,958 --> 00:23:25,041
His Grace.

385
00:23:25,625 --> 00:23:27,290
Eddie.

386
00:23:27,291 --> 00:23:28,416
Hear, hear.

387
00:23:29,666 --> 00:23:31,000
- Right, shall we?
- Yes.

388
00:23:33,416 --> 00:23:35,165
Why didn't you drink?

389
00:23:35,166 --> 00:23:38,457
Ah. Not really the done thing
when it's about oneself, eh?

390
00:23:38,458 --> 00:23:40,082
We've already broken with custom.

391
00:23:40,083 --> 00:23:43,540
It's hardly the done thing to propose
a toast before we've even sat down.

392
00:23:43,541 --> 00:23:45,290
You think Cico's uncouth?

393
00:23:45,291 --> 00:23:48,290
Lord Hawthorne was merely,
um, highlighting the differences

394
00:23:48,291 --> 00:23:50,500
between Italian
and English tradition, right?

395
00:23:54,041 --> 00:23:55,290
He is uncouth.

396
00:23:55,291 --> 00:23:57,625
I think the gamekeeper got it wrong.

397
00:23:58,125 --> 00:24:01,374
If it was up to me, I would give you
the best dressed award.

398
00:24:01,375 --> 00:24:05,207
Cico's idea of class is giving me
a stripy lion for my birthday.

399
00:24:05,208 --> 00:24:07,125
- You mean a tiger?
- Whatever.

400
00:24:07,958 --> 00:24:10,624
- Sabrina, darling. Will you excuse me?
- Yes, of course.

401
00:24:10,625 --> 00:24:13,999
- Aisha, can you join me for something?
- Right behind you.

402
00:24:14,000 --> 00:24:16,333
Not having much luck
with the birds today, are you, Cico?

403
00:24:18,208 --> 00:24:20,333
- How did you two meet?
- At a party.

404
00:24:21,000 --> 00:24:22,457
He was selling cocaine.

405
00:24:22,458 --> 00:24:25,082
Well, at least that's not a cliché.
Were you buying?

406
00:24:25,083 --> 00:24:26,333
God, no.

407
00:24:27,750 --> 00:24:31,708
I know you think he's gauche and clumsy,
but he's ambitious.

408
00:24:32,208 --> 00:24:33,832
A trait I find attractive.

409
00:24:33,833 --> 00:24:34,791
Hm.

410
00:24:35,791 --> 00:24:38,375
The clumsiness of that ambition
is a work in progress though.

411
00:24:38,875 --> 00:24:43,000
You seem to elicit his insecurity,
and he can be volatile.

412
00:24:44,500 --> 00:24:47,499
What about you, Eddie?
What are your ambitions?

413
00:24:47,500 --> 00:24:48,541
Mine?

414
00:24:49,125 --> 00:24:52,374
Well, how do you know
I'm not a feckless aristocrat

415
00:24:52,375 --> 00:24:54,291
willing to live off his dwindling asset?

416
00:24:55,416 --> 00:24:57,833
'Cause there's nothing feckless
about you, Your Grace.

417
00:24:59,750 --> 00:25:02,582
Well, you give me
the benefit of the doubt.

418
00:25:02,583 --> 00:25:03,791
I do.

419
00:25:25,000 --> 00:25:26,375
Sorry, can I help you?

420
00:25:31,583 --> 00:25:32,708
I don't know.

421
00:25:33,458 --> 00:25:34,416
Can you?

422
00:25:35,000 --> 00:25:36,250
Are you lost?

423
00:25:37,791 --> 00:25:40,416
Yes. I think I'm in a dream.

424
00:25:41,833 --> 00:25:44,291
This is the most beautiful machine
I've ever seen.

425
00:25:48,041 --> 00:25:49,041
Well, then.

426
00:25:50,250 --> 00:25:52,040
Let me wake you up from this dream.

427
00:25:52,041 --> 00:25:53,166
Mm-hmm.

428
00:25:59,541 --> 00:26:01,582
- Darling.
- Darling.

429
00:26:01,583 --> 00:26:02,707
Bag, sir?

430
00:26:02,708 --> 00:26:05,999
Jolly kind of you, Mr. Lawrence.
Sorry I'm late.

431
00:26:06,000 --> 00:26:08,124
- Sorry for this ghastly attire.
- Mm.

432
00:26:08,125 --> 00:26:11,250
A couple of complications at work.
Have I missed my boy?

433
00:26:11,750 --> 00:26:13,416
I've literally just put him down.

434
00:26:15,416 --> 00:26:17,624
Come. Come, you must be famished.

435
00:26:17,625 --> 00:26:22,957
I'm not going anywhere until you tell me
who that wonderful vehicle belongs to.

436
00:26:22,958 --> 00:26:26,208
But after all that, it turned out
he was an erotic dancer from Corinth.

437
00:26:27,500 --> 00:26:28,958
I know who you are.

438
00:26:30,375 --> 00:26:32,832
Do you know how many bodies
of the Mark VI Drophead Coupé

439
00:26:32,833 --> 00:26:34,499
were actually built by Franay?

440
00:26:34,500 --> 00:26:36,249
Um, uh…

441
00:26:36,250 --> 00:26:38,250
- Not very many?
- Eighteen.

442
00:26:38,750 --> 00:26:41,500
How do you do, Lord Hawthorne?
I'm Nanny Asante.

443
00:26:42,125 --> 00:26:43,540
But you can call me Nanny.

444
00:26:43,541 --> 00:26:46,249
Your car is a jewel. An absolute jewel.

445
00:26:46,250 --> 00:26:48,207
Oh, thank you, Nanny.

446
00:26:48,208 --> 00:26:50,749
In which case,
you must take her for a spin tomorrow.

447
00:26:50,750 --> 00:26:53,083
I couldn't. I shouldn't.

448
00:26:54,458 --> 00:26:55,541
But I must.

449
00:26:57,541 --> 00:26:58,916
Where did you two meet?

450
00:26:59,666 --> 00:27:02,207
Nanny is a rather outstanding soldier.

451
00:27:02,208 --> 00:27:06,040
He was awarded the military cross
for his extraordinary bravery in combat.

452
00:27:06,041 --> 00:27:08,249
They met at
our Sandhurst Commissioners Ball.

453
00:27:08,250 --> 00:27:11,166
- Nanny and I were commissioned together.
- I was Eddie's date.

454
00:27:12,625 --> 00:27:14,916
The '95, sir. Your favorite.

455
00:27:15,416 --> 00:27:19,707
Thank you, Mr. Lawrence. Be a love
and also make sure the jacket is pressed.

456
00:27:19,708 --> 00:27:21,082
Very good.

457
00:27:21,083 --> 00:27:23,207
You should think about children, Edward.

458
00:27:23,208 --> 00:27:26,040
Don't want the estate to fall
into the hands of that brother of yours.

459
00:27:26,041 --> 00:27:28,541
- Where is he, by the way?
- Well, he's recuperating.

460
00:27:29,333 --> 00:27:31,333
Oh, what was the poison?

461
00:27:31,958 --> 00:27:35,250
Ah, too many and too varied
to discuss at dinner, I'm afraid.

462
00:27:46,500 --> 00:27:49,624
Well, people think
that he just looks after the horses.

463
00:27:49,625 --> 00:27:54,416
But in actual fact, the King's Equerry
is the aide-de-camp to the sovereign.

464
00:27:55,000 --> 00:27:58,707
Nanny is basically
the King of England's right-hand man.

465
00:27:58,708 --> 00:28:00,750
Don't tell my boss.
He'll try and poach him.

466
00:28:01,791 --> 00:28:05,833
Anything the English king likes, he wants.

467
00:28:09,208 --> 00:28:11,207
Well, I'm sure they'd get on like--

468
00:28:11,208 --> 00:28:12,665
Wine. Wine, wine, wine.

469
00:28:12,666 --> 00:28:16,207
Can I offer you a new glass?
It's a new wine. We're on to the Mouton.

470
00:28:16,208 --> 00:28:17,791
- And I was…
- Right.

471
00:28:18,416 --> 00:28:22,707
- What happened to the Masseto?
- I'm afraid it's finished. It's a 1989.

472
00:28:22,708 --> 00:28:23,624
Ah.

473
00:28:23,625 --> 00:28:28,791
Yes. It's not at all musty,
if that's what you're worried about.

474
00:28:39,958 --> 00:28:40,791
Right.

475
00:29:04,375 --> 00:29:05,625
The wine is corked.

476
00:29:08,625 --> 00:29:09,666
Mr. Lawrence.

477
00:29:10,416 --> 00:29:12,040
Do you know,

478
00:29:12,041 --> 00:29:16,790
my father-in-law, he once sent back
six bottles on the spin.

479
00:29:16,791 --> 00:29:22,415
But it turned out that he'd had a stroke
and he'd lost all sense of taste.

480
00:29:22,416 --> 00:29:25,165
Well, it did come back
a couple of days later,

481
00:29:25,166 --> 00:29:28,958
but it was £20,000
straight down the <i>toilette</i>.

482
00:29:30,333 --> 00:29:33,333
Maybe you've had a stroke, Cico.
How about that?

483
00:29:33,833 --> 00:29:34,833
You're right.

484
00:29:36,166 --> 00:29:37,250
The wine is corked.

485
00:29:39,458 --> 00:29:41,333
Would you care to select another with me?

486
00:29:45,000 --> 00:29:47,291
There's no Italian wine here.

487
00:29:48,583 --> 00:29:52,041
